  "query_preview": "..Slow moving trough continues to impact the region\ntoday with more showers and occasional thunderstorms moving across\nthe state. By Tuesday a cold core upper level system drops into\nthe state bringing colder but a bit drier air than we've seen the\nlast few days. Many normally dry washes are running at this time \nand some local creeks and rivers are showing some elevated flow, \nbut the greatest heavy rain threat is likely behind us with that \ndrier air moving in. Will go ahead and let the Flood Watch expire \nat 5pm tonight. \n\nShower and thunderstorm chances continue Tuesday but will begin to\nend from west to east Tuesday night into Wednesday morning as the\nLow moves off to the northeast. Expect breezy conditions to\ncontinue as the polar jet wraps around the base of this trough. With\nthe aforementioned cold air being brought with this Low, \nelevations above 6500-7500ft along the Rim, Kaibab Plateau and \nWhite Mountains could see some snow mix in Tuesday night/Wednesday\nmorning. No accumulations are expected except maybe the tip top \nof the San Francisco Peaks. The coldest low temperatures so far\nthis Fall are expected early Wednesday morning which will give\nhigher elevations their first hard freeze and mid elevations their\nfirst significant frost. \n\nAfter Wednesday afternoon when the last shower chances leave the\nstate along the Arizona/Utah border expect the first real break\nwe've seen in a few days. Things dry out but the colder air\nremains in the area so temperatures will be about 10 degrees below\nmid October averages.  \n\nThings become a bit uncertain for Friday and Saturday as models\ndiverge on what's coming up. The trough moving away from our area\ntakes a positive tilt and pinches off an upper level Low to our\nsouthwest located under an incoming West Coast ridge giving us a \nRex Block pattern. Some models show this upper level Low bringing \nup some moisture from the south to give a chance for some showers \nand storms along the higher elevation, some models don't. For \nright now low grade POPs have been introduced for both those days,\nbut this could change with newer model runs.",
